---
import ServiceLayout from '../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import { faqs } from '../config/faqs';

const description =
	'UniFi Protect camera systems with local recording and no mandatory cloud video subscription. Placement, coverage, and remote viewing planned for the property.';
---

<ServiceLayout
	title="UniFi Security Cameras"
	description={description}
	eyebrow="Security"
	heading="Your security footage. Under your control."
	lede="Cameras are useful when they capture the right view and keep it. UniFi Protect records on equipment at the property, without a mandatory cloud video subscription."
	crumb="Security Cameras"
	serviceName="UniFi Protect security camera installation"
	serviceType="Security camera installation"
	faqs={faqs.cameras}
	ctaLabel="Plan Your Camera System"
	ctaTitle="Plan your camera system."
	ctaText="Entrances, drives, outbuildings, and the views you actually need are more useful than a camera count."
	related={[
		{ href: '/unifi-networking/', label: 'UniFi networking', text: 'Cameras are only as solid as the network under them.' },
		{ href: '/network-security/', label: 'Network security', text: 'Keep recorders and cameras off the everyday network.' },
		{ href: '/wireless-bridges/', label: 'Wireless bridges', text: 'Cover a building that is awkward to cable.' },
	]}
>
	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="problem-heading">Cloud-only cameras rent you your own history.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					A consumer camera that stores video only in someone else’s cloud is convenient until the subscription
					lapses, the company changes terms, or the clip you needed aged out of the plan. The lens is on your
					house. The recording is not.
				</p>
				<p>
					Local recording reverses that. A UniFi Protect recorder or a capable console keeps footage on equipment at
					the property. You decide retention by storage and settings, not by a monthly video tier.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream" aria-labelledby="plan-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="plan-heading">Coverage is a placement problem.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					High-resolution cameras still miss what they are not pointed at. Doors, drives, gates, the side of a
					workshop, and the counter of a small business each ask for a different view. Indoor and outdoor cameras,
					including doorbell cameras, are chosen for those views.
				</p>
				<p>
					Power, cable, and the recorder’s capacity are part of the same plan. A camera that cannot be reached
					reliably, or that fills the disk in a weekend, is not a finished system.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="features-heading">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2 id="features-heading">What a Protect system can include.</h2>
			<ul class="index-list">
				<li>
					<strong>Local recording</strong>
					<p>Video stored on a Protect recorder or console at the property. No mandatory cloud video subscription.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Useful detections</strong>
					<p>
						On supported hardware: person detection, vehicle detection, smart search, and license plate recognition.
						These are not features of every UniFi camera.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The right camera</strong>
					<p>Doorbell, indoor, and outdoor models, specified for the light and the distance of the shot.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Remote viewing</strong>
					<p>Secure access for the people who should have it, designed with the gateway and the recorder — not left wide open.</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
			<aside class="note">
				<p>
					Advanced detection capabilities depend on the selected UniFi camera and Protect hardware. A system is not
					described as having facial recognition, license plate recognition, or every AI feature unless the chosen
					equipment supports it.
				</p>
			</aside>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="why-heading">Cameras belong on their own part of the network.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					Recording equipment should not sit on the same open network as every laptop. The camera segment, the
					viewer permissions, and the path back to the gateway are designed together. That is also why a camera
					project often starts as a network project.
				</p>
				<p>
					Solutions are built with UniFi Protect. This is not a claim of an authorized dealer or certified-installer
					relationship.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</ServiceLayout>
